# Pulse — web (pulse-web) environment.
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. Never commit `.env*` (only
# this `.env.example` is committed, per CLAUDE.md § 4).
#
# These are the PUBLIC (NEXT_PUBLIC_*) client-side variables only. They
# are inlined into the browser bundle at build time, so they must contain
# NO secrets — server secrets (DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET,
# WEBHOOK_SIGNING_KEY, ...) live on the NestJS `pulse-api` side (ADR-006),
# never here.

# Base URL of the NestJS API (monitors CRUD, auth, public read endpoints).
# Local dev: the API runs on its own port (backend Task 1.1 picks 3080).
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3080

# Base URL of the live SSE stream (the `EventSource` target for the status
# board — `GET /api/stream`, ADR-003). Local dev points at the same API
# origin as NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SSE_URL=http://localhost:3080

# Canonical public site URL (metadataBase, OG, sitemap). Local dev default.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3081

# Slug of the example public status page the landing CTA + the sitemap link to
# (the seeded demo page, `/status/<slug>`). Read by `src/lib/env.ts` with a
# safe `demo` default, so this is optional; documented per CLAUDE.md § 4 (every
# read var is documented). Must match the slug the backend seeds for the demo
# public page (`seed-public-page.ts` uses `demo`).
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_STATUS_SLUG=demo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v2 single-origin reverse-proxy intent (ADR-006, the meld precedent).
#
# In the deployed topology the `pulse-web` Fly app reverse-proxies the
# NestJS `pulse-api` (the meld Next-proxies-API pattern, including the
# catch-all transport-encoding-header strip). The browser then talks to a
# SINGLE origin: the dashboard `fetch`es and the `EventSource` SSE stream
# are BOTH same-origin, so:
#
#   - `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_SSE_URL` collapse to the
#     deployed web origin (or empty / same-origin relative paths), and
#   - the strict CSP `connect-src 'self'` in next.config.ts is sufficient
#     without widening it to a separate API host.
#
# Keep both vars distinct in local dev (two ports) but expect them to point
# at the same origin in production. If a deploy ever points the browser at
# a DISTINCT API origin, that origin must be added to `connect-src` in
# next.config.ts explicitly — do not pre-emptively widen it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
